{
 "_note": "One American English word, several Italian ones. Each set lists vocabulary.json terms (exact 'term' strings) under the English word they share. A set with a 'note' groups words the rules keep apart, and the note says how; 'topic' is the review that teaches the difference. Checked by validate_data.py.",
 "sets": [
  {
   "en": "stop",
   "terms": [
    "fermata",
    "sosta",
    "arresto",
    "fermarsi"
   ],
   "note": "Three different things in Italian law. A fermata is a brief interruption of the journey, and the driver stays with the vehicle. A sosta is a longer stop, and the driver may leave the vehicle; English usually calls it parking. An arresto is a halt the traffic imposes, such as at a red light or in a queue. Fermarsi is the everyday verb and can mean any of the three.",
   "topic": 12
  },
  {
   "en": "parking",
   "terms": [
    "sosta",
    "parcheggio",
    "stazionamento"
   ],
   "note": "Sosta is the act of parking. Parcheggio is usually the place, a parking area set aside for it. Stazionamento appears mainly in freno di stazionamento, the parking brake.",
   "topic": 12
  },
  {
   "en": "road",
   "terms": [
    "strada",
    "carreggiata"
   ],
   "note": "A strada is the whole road, including sidewalks and shoulders. The carreggiata is only the part vehicles drive on, so the shoulder (banchina) and the sidewalk are part of the strada but not of the carreggiata.",
   "topic": 16
  },
  {
   "en": "shoulder",
   "terms": [
    "banchina",
    "la corsia di emergenza"
   ],
   "note": "An American calls both of these the shoulder. The banchina is the strip beside the carriageway on an ordinary road. The corsia di emergenza is the emergency lane of an autostrada or a main extra-urban road, meant for emergency stops and emergency vehicles.",
   "topic": 16
  },
  {
   "en": "highway",
   "terms": [
    "autostrada",
    "strade extraurbane principali",
    "strade extraurbane secondarie"
   ],
   "note": "Three different road classes with different limits. The autostrada is the toll motorway (130 km/h). The strada extraurbana principale is a divided highway without at-grade junctions (110 km/h). A strada extraurbana secondaria is an ordinary two-lane road outside town (90 km/h).",
   "topic": 11
  },
  {
   "en": "to pass",
   "terms": [
    "sorpassare",
    "superare",
    "incrociare"
   ],
   "note": "Sorpassare is to overtake a vehicle, the maneuver the overtaking rules govern. Superare is the general verb: to get past something, to exceed a limit, or to cross a line, and it is also used for overtaking. Incrociare is to meet a vehicle coming the other way, which is never overtaking.",
   "topic": 10
  },
  {
   "en": "motor vehicle",
   "terms": [
    "autoveicolo",
    "autovettura",
    "autocarro",
    "veicoli a motore"
   ],
   "note": "Not interchangeable. An autovettura is a car. An autocarro is a truck. An autoveicolo is the whole class of motor vehicles with at least four wheels, cars and trucks included. Veicoli a motore is wider still: any vehicle with an engine, mopeds and motorcycles included.",
   "topic": 16
  },
  {
   "en": "must / should",
   "terms": [
    "è obbligatorio",
    "bisogna",
    "occorre",
    "si deve",
    "è opportuno",
    "è consigliabile",
    "conviene"
   ],
   "note": "The first four state a duty, and the last three only give advice. A statement can turn advice into a duty, or a duty into advice, so check which kind of word it uses before answering."
  },
  {
   "en": "offense",
   "terms": [
    "infrazione",
    "violazione",
    "reato"
   ],
   "note": "Infrazione and violazione are usually administrative violations, punished with a fine and sometimes points. A reato is a crime, handled by a criminal court, such as driving with a blood alcohol level above 0.8 g/l or leaving the scene of an accident with injuries.",
   "topic": 23
  },
  {
